WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.821
Authentication and authorization are different.

2
00:00:02.821 --> 00:00:05.051
Authentication identifies the caller.

3
00:00:05.051 --> 00:00:08.134
Authorization decides what the caller may access or do.

4
00:00:08.134 --> 00:00:15.087
ORM access must include authorization because a query can be technically
safe and still return data the caller should not see.

5
00:00:15.087 --> 00:00:22.237
Every sensitive data operation should verify the subject, action,
resource, and context before returning or changing records.

6
00:00:22.237 --> 00:00:25.254
Object-level authorization is central to ORM safety.

7
00:00:25.254 --> 00:00:29.649
A user may be allowed to access a path or endpoint, but not every record
behind it.

8
00:00:29.649 --> 00:00:37.455
Queries should filter by the current user, role, tenant, organization,
project, permission, workflow state, or ownership as appropriate.

9
00:00:37.455 --> 00:00:43.489
List endpoints are especially important because overbroad queries can
quietly expose many records at once.

10
00:00:43.489 --> 00:00:48.540
Detail endpoints, updates, deletes, and exports need the same resource-
specific checks.

11
00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:54.116
Tenant and organization boundaries should be built into query patterns
rather than added by memory.

12
00:00:54.116 --> 00:01:00.478
Multi-tenant applications need consistent filters so one tenant cannot
read or modify another tenant's records.

13
00:01:00.478 --> 00:01:05.201
Support and administrative access should have explicit rules, logging, and
review.

14
00:01:05.201 --> 00:01:09.858
Hidden buttons, hidden routes, and client-side filtering are not access
controls.

15
00:01:09.858 --> 00:01:14.647
Server-side ORM queries should return only what the current context is
allowed to see.

16
00:01:14.647 --> 00:01:18.779
Preventing overbroad queries is partly architecture and partly testing.

17
00:01:18.779 --> 00:01:25.076
Sensitive repository functions should require context, not allow callers
to fetch everything and filter later.

18
00:01:25.076 --> 00:01:32.095
Tests should confirm denied cases, cross-tenant isolation, role limits,
object ownership, and administrative exceptions.

19
00:01:32.095 --> 00:01:40.360
When authorization-aware filters are embedded in the data-access layer,
developers are less likely to forget them in new routes, jobs, and
reports.
